  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\nEarly this morning, high pressure centered over Lake Michigan \nis delivering a crisp, fall-like morning across central \nIllinois, with temperatures predominantly in the 50s under light\neasterly winds. To the west, a closed upper-level low is \nlifting across the Canadian Rockies with surface cyclogenesis \nongoing over the northern Great Plains. An associated warm front\nis advancing northward through the Great Plains, supporting \nconvective activity focused across the mid and upper Missouri \nRiver Valley.\n\nThis convection will largely remain west of the local area \ntoday as it lifts into the Upper Midwest. However, there is a \nminor (10-15%) chance for a few lingering showers or an isolated\nthunderstorm to clip far northwestern counties north of the \nIllinois River late morning into early afternoon. Otherwise, dry\nconditions will prevail today. Southeasterly winds will \nstrengthen and become breezy as the surface pressure gradient \ntightens from the west, with gusts of 15 to 25 mph expected this\nafternoon. Temperatures will be near seasonal norms, with highs\naround 80 along the I-74 corridor and in the low-to-mid 80s \nacross the remainder of central Illinois.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...\nThe northern Plains surface low will continue to deepen while \nlifting across the Canadian Prairies toward Hudson Bay Tuesday. \nA trailing cold front will sweep across the Upper Midwest, with \nits southern extension reaching central Illinois Tuesday \nafternoon. Widespread showers and thunderstorms are expected \nalong and ahead of this boundary, persisting into Wednesday as \nthe frontal zone becomes oriented parallel to mid/upper-level \nflow and stalls directly across central Illinois.\n\nSevere Weather Potential:\nThe severe threat warrants close monitoring. Deep layer shear of\n30-40 kt, enhanced by an upper-level jet streak over the Upper\nMidwest, will overlap the frontal boundary. GFS guidance\nsuggests pockets of 1000-1500 J/kg MLCAPE developing Tuesday,\nincreasing to 1500-2000 J/kg on Wednesday. This thermodynamic\nand kinematic environment will support organized convection\ncapable of isolated severe wind gusts. Colorado State University\n(CSU) machine learning probabilities indicate a 15-30% severe\nrisk Tuesday and 5-15% Wednesday across central Illinois. The\nStorm Prediction Center (SPC) has a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5)\nfor most of central Illinois Tuesday and a risk area may be\nintroduced Wednesday once forecast details come into better\nfocus.\n\nHeavy Rainfall Threat:\nIn addition to severe potential, heavy rainfall and localized \nflooding will be concerns as multiple convective rounds train \nalong the lingering frontal boundary. NBM 48-hour mean QPF \nending Wednesday night indicates 1.00+ inch amounts north of a \nHavana to Bloomington line, with 2.00+ inch QPF contour edging \ninto far northwestern sections just north of Galesburg.\n\nTemperature Disparity:\nA sharp thermal gradient will develop across the frontal zone\nTuesday and Wednesday. North of the boundary, cloud cover and\nprecipitation will cap highs in the lower-to-mid 80s. South of\nthe boundary, filtered sunshine will allow temperatures to climb\ninto the low-to-mid 90s with elevated humidity as dew points\nclimb back into the lower 70s. Triple digit heat indices will be\nthe result, especially along and south of the I-72 corridor.\n\nEnd of the Week and Weekend...\nUnsettled weather will persist into Thursday and Friday as the \nbaroclinic zone remains draped across northern and central \nIllinois. Global models (specifically ECMWF and GFS) have begun \nto converge on a solution keeping the boundary anchored across \nportions of central and northern Illinois. Consequently, daily \nchances for convection will continue alongside lingering \n90-degree heat across southern portions of the forecast area. \nLittle pattern change is anticipated heading into the weekend, \nmaintaining periodic shower/thunderstorm chances and a strong \nnorth-to-south temperature contrast across the region.",
